<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">County Wexford</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">County <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wexford">Wexford</a> is a county in the province of Leinster in south-eastern Ireland, named after its county town, Wexford, and bordered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irish-sea">Irish Sea</a> and St George's Channel. Its coast was among the first parts of Ireland taken by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-normans">Anglo-Normans</a> from 1169. In 1798 it was the main centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/society-of-united-irishmen">United Irishmen</a>'s rebellion, with battles at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-ross">New Ross</a> and Vinegar Hill.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The rising of 1641 began a decade of war involving the Catholic Confederation of Kilkenny, royalists, Scottish Covenanters and English parliamentarians, ended by Oliver Cromwell’s campaign of 1649–1650, with the storming of Drogheda and <b>Wexford</b>, and the completion of the conquest by 1653.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ireland">Kingdom of Ireland · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Lyte was a curate in <b>County Wexford</b> from 1815 to 1818, where he often preached at the church in Killurin and came under the lasting influence of its rector, the Reverend Abraham Swanne; he was later vicar of All Saints' Church in Brixham, Devon.</blockquote><a href="/songs/abide-with-me">Abide with Me</a></li><li><blockquote>The Wexford Carol, also called the Enniscorthy Carol (in Irish Carúl Loch Garman or Carúl Inis Córthaidh), is a traditional Irish Christmas carol that comes from Enniscorthy in <b>County Wexford</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/wexford-carol">Wexford Carol</a></li><li><blockquote>Ptolemy's Geography of about 150 lists the Brigantes in south-eastern Ireland, usually placed in the area of modern <b>Wexford</b> and Waterford, near the Coriondi and the Usdiae.</blockquote><a href="/state/brigantes-ireland">Brigantes (Ireland)</a></li><li><blockquote>The Coriondi appear in Ptolemy's Geography of about 150, placed in the interior of south-eastern Ireland, roughly in modern Counties Kilkenny, Carlow or <b>Wexford</b>, near the Brigantes (Ireland) and the Usdiae.</blockquote><a href="/state/coriondi">Coriondi</a></li><li><blockquote>The five sons of William Marshal all died without heirs, and in 1247 the liberty was divided among his daughters' heirs, forming Carlow, Kilkenny, <b>Wexford</b>, Kildare and part of Laois.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-leinster">Lordship of Leinster</a></li></ul></section>
